# Basebase Framework - AI Assistant Rules

## IMPORTANT: LEARN FROM PATTERNS IN OTHER APPS and FUNCTIONS

When the user asks you to add functionality to their specific app, you should first search through the existing repository of working apps at `apps/*` to look for working exmaples of the functionlity that you need to build, and copy the patterns as closely as possible. You
can also read detailed documentation in `docs/DEVELOPERS_GUIDE.md`.

Similarly, when the user asks you to create custom server (cloud) functions for their apps,
please search through the existing server functions to look for patterns that you can copy.
You can also read detailed documentation in `docs/FUNCTION_AUTHORING.md`.

## CRITICAL: DO NOT EDIT FRAMEWORK CODE

**ONLY modify files in the `/app` directory.**

### ✅ You CAN edit:

- `app/**/*` - All application code
- `app/components/**` - React components
- `app/stores/**` - Zustand stores
- `app/schema.js` - Data structure definitions
- `app/app.jsx` - Main entry point
- `functions/*` - This is where custom server (cloud) functions are defined for apps

### ❌ NEVER edit:

- `framework/**/*` - Core framework code
- `scripts/**/*` - Build/deployment scripts
- `vite.config.js` - Build configuration
- `package.json` - Dependencies (unless adding new app dependencies)

### Why?

- Framework code is shared across all apps
- Only `/app` folder contents are committed to Firestore
- Changes outside `/app` will work locally but FAIL in production
- Framework updates are managed by the platform team

### Workflow:

1. User asks for new functionality or bug fixes in their app (specifying the `appId`)
2. Agent looks for similar working examples in the repo as mentioned above
3. Agent implements changes by creating/editng files in `/app/appId` or `/functions` folders ONLY
4. User tests locally with `npm run dev`, viewing app on `https://<appId>.localhost:3000`
5. Ask user to deploy/publish with `npm run app:commit <appId>` and `npm run function:commit <filename.js>` (custom function changes only)
6. Custom file contents are compiled, concatenated, and uploaded to Firestore

**If you're asked to modify anything that is off limits, politely decline and explain this restriction.**
